<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gesta Hungarorum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1200</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gesta Hungarorum is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> chronicle of the Hungarian conquest of the Carpathian Basin, written around 1200 by an anonymous notary of a King Béla. It describes local rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glad">Glad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menumorut">Menumorut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelou">Gelou</a> resisting the Hungarians around 900. Because these figures appear in no other source, its reliability is much debated.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gesta-hungarorum.jpg" alt="Gesta Hungarorum" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gesta_Hungarorum_Anonymous.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Gesta Hungarorum, or The Deeds of the Hungarians, is the earliest Hungarian chronicle to survive. It was written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> by an unidentified author, called Anonymus in scholarship since the first Hungarian translation of 1790. In the opening sentence he describes himself as 'P who is called magister, and sometime notary of the most glorious Béla, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> of fond memory'. Four Hungarian kings bore the name, and most historians identify him as Béla III, who died in 1196.</p><p>Its sources included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isidore-of-seville">Isidore of Seville</a>'s Etymologiae, the Exordia Scythica, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regino-of-prum">Regino of Prüm</a>'s Chronicon and early medieval romances of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. He also used folk songs and ballads, and knew a version of the late 11th-century Hungarian Chronicle. The work survives in one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of 24 folios in Gothic minuscule, made in the middle or second half of the 13th century. It is now in the Széchényi National Library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Gesta has a prologue and 57 chapters. Its main subject is the conquest of the Carpathian Basin at the turn of the 9th and 10th centuries. The first seven chapters describe the Hungarians' supposed homeland, called Scythia or Dentumoger, and trace their ancestors to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>.</p><p>The next chapters follow the Hungarians past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> and describe their fights with the Rus' and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>. Anonymus names opponents of the Hungarians that no other source mentions, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glad">Glad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menumorut">Menumorut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelou">Gelou</a>, and does not name those known from sources written around 900. One theory holds that he took these names from place names.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The single <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> suggests the chronicle was not much read in its own time or later. Later chronicles did not use it, which Gyula Kristó takes to mean that contemporaries knew Anonymus had invented most details of his account of the conquest. Scholars at Halle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a> soon doubted it, pointing to its anachronistic account of the Rus' principalities, and in the 1780s Juraj Sklenár dismissed it because it never mentions Great Moravia.</p><p>Romanian historians have presented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelou">Gelou</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glad">Glad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menumorut">Menumorut</a> as Romanian rulers, taking them as proof of Romanian polities at the time of the conquest. Modern views are mixed. Alexandru Madgearu judges the work generally credible despite chronological mistakes, while Macartney called it the most misleading of the early Hungarian texts. Dennis Deletant concludes that the case for and against Gelou and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlachs">Vlachs</a> cannot be proven.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gesta Hungarorum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its chronicles, the <b>Gesta Hungarorum</b> written around 1200 by an anonymous notary of a King Béla, probably Béla III, and Simon of Kézai's Gesta of the 1280s, shaped Hungarian historical memory for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>He had been baptised by the Orthodox rite at Vidin, and the <b>Gesta Hungarorum</b> makes him a descendant of Glad of the Duchy of Glad.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-ahtum">Duchy of Ahtum</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Gesta Hungarorum</b>, written around 1200, is the only source.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-gelou">Duchy of Gelou</a></li><li><blockquote>The main source is the <b>Gesta Hungarorum</b>, written by an anonymous notary around 1200.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-glad">Duchy of Glad</a></li><li><blockquote>Menumorut is known only from the <b>Gesta Hungarorum</b>, written around 1200.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-menumorut">Duchy of Menumorut</a></li></ul></section>
